-- title:
--   Isotypic modules stay isotypic on each of several commuting generating subgroups
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a finite group, $\mathbb{C}[G]$ its complex group algebra, and $M$ a $\mathbb{C}[G]$-module (no finite-dimensionality is assumed). A module over a ring is *isotypic* if any two of its simple submodules are isomorphic; in particular the zero module is isotypic. For a subgroup $H\le G$, the inclusion $H\hookrightarrow G$ induces the algebra homomorphism $\iota_H:\mathbb{C}[H]\to\mathbb{C}[G]$, $\sum_h a_h\,h\mapsto\sum_h a_h\,h$, and $M$ becomes a $\mathbb{C}[H]$-module by restriction: $s\cdot x=\iota_H(s)\cdot x$.
--
--   Let $(H_i)_{i\in I}$ be a family of subgroups of $G$, indexed by an arbitrary set $I$, such that
--
--   1. the subgroups $H_i$ together generate $G$, and
--   2. elements of different members commute: $gk=kg$ whenever $i\neq j$, $g\in H_i$ and $k\in H_j$.
--
--   Then, for every index $i\in I$,
--
--   $$
--   M\ \text{isotypic as a }\mathbb{C}[G]\text{-module}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad M\ \text{isotypic as a }\mathbb{C}[H_i]\text{-module}.
--   $$
--
--   In the language of representations: if a representation of $G$ is a direct sum of copies of a single irreducible representation, then its restriction to each commuting factor $H_i$ is again a sum of copies of a single irreducible representation of $H_i$. This allows an isotypic piece of a representation of a group generated by commuting subgroups (for example permutation groups of disjoint blocks) to be analysed one factor at a time.
--
--   **Formalization Note** "Isotypic" is Mathlib's `IsIsotypic`. The restricted module structure is `restrictGroupModule (H i)`, i.e. `Module.compHom` along `MonoidAlgebra.mapDomainAlgHom ℂ ℂ (H i).subtype`, installed with `letI` inside the statement. Generation is the lattice condition `⨆ i, H i = ⊤`.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/RepresentationTheory/PartialPermutation/Restriction.lean#L56-L112

